      "writtenOn": "2026-08-17T11:14:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why do we need to control the PAM ordering?\n\nAs I understand it, the current PAM order is local authentication followed by remote authentication. However, if the requirement is that a particular user exists only on the remote authentication server, why do we need to perform local authentication first?\n\nConsidering that a BMC typically has a very limited number of local users, even if we perform local authentication first and then fall back to remote authentication, what is the actual additional time incurred by the local authentication step?\n\nIt would be useful to understand the performance impact before adding controls for PAM ordering or is there any other impact?",
      "revId": "267e824dcabb3a26c7bb27b4d34b897ed2593634",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fe0d55f4_f029da1b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002630
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-18T07:57:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e if the requirement is that a particular user exists only on the remote authentication server, why do we need to perform local authentication first?\n\nExactly, there is no reason to. as of now every LDAP-only user login unnecessarily goes through pam_unix.so failure before reaching pam_ldap.so. RemoteFirst eliminates that wasted attempt. Admins in LDAP-heavy environments can set RemoteFirst and avoid the overhead entirely.\n\n\u003e what is the actual additional time incurred by the local authentication step?\n\nUsing LDAP user creds,\nLocalFirst: Redfish login total 306.03ms (server-side auth: 268.86ms)\nRemoteFirst: Redfish login total 202.18ms (server-side auth: 166.15ms)\n~104ms overhead per LDAP user login under LocalFirst, entirely from pam_unix.so failure before falling through to pam_ldap.so.\n\n\u003e It would be useful to understand the performance impact before adding controls for PAM ordering or is there any other impact?\n\nYes, When the same username exists in both local and LDAP with different passwords, as of now local password always wins regardless of LDAP. In environments where LDAP is the authoritative source, this is the wrong behavior. LoginPriority fixes the authentication order, but the privilege resolution for overlapping usernames in getUserInfo is a pre-existing issue and will be addressed as a separate patch.",
      "parentUuid": "4a4361a1_e5a66987",
      "revId": "267e824dcabb3a26c7bb27b4d34b897ed2593634",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
